马三退五 { [%emt 0:08:03] [%think 45569] [%out 46042] [%in 2]
[%cacheread 12171] [%cachewrite 687] [%dropped 1] The black knight moving to e6
poses no direct threat. The real issues are on my side: - **The rook is tied
down.** Black's rook on g3 attacks my knight on g2. That knight is protected
only by my rook on h2, so the rook can't leave. - **The edge pawn is loose.**
With my rook on h2, the knight's path to i3 is blocked, so the i3 pawn has no
protection. Losing it is minor. I'm up a knight and a cannon, so I'll
consolidate first. Knight g2 retreats to e1 (马三退五): 1. **The rook is
freed.** The knight is no longer under attack, and the rook on h2 is free to
join the attack. 2. **Weak points get covered.** From e1 the knight guards the
g0 elephant, the c2 knight, and the f3 and d3 squares. If Black's rook comes
down to capture g0 or goes to f3, the knight takes it. 3. **Next I offer a rook
trade.** I can play 车二平三 (rook to g2), protected by the e1 knight, to face
Black's rook. If the rooks come off, my extra knight and cannon should be
enough to win comfortably. 4. **Black's rook has no good targets.** If it takes
the i3 pawn, then i1 only hits the h1 cannon, which the rook protects. i0 only
hits the g0 elephant, which the knight protects. Going to i2 lets my rook
capture it. } 车７平９ { [%emt 0:00:40] [%think 983] [%out 1021] [%in 1535]
